that keeps gender identity from parents (rungs 1–2). It does not choose between telling parents when they ask, notifying them, or requiring their permission (rungs 3, 4 and 5).\n\nHer two Aye votes are coded but cannot seat a chair at this level:\n- **H.R. 5 (2023, Parents Bill of Rights Act):** a bill with many unrelated parts. Her op-ed praises the bill as a whole but does not tie her vote to its notification or consent clauses.\n- **H.R. 2616 (2026, PROTECT Kids Act):** it requires parental consent only for changes on school forms, and only for elementary and middle-grade students. That is narrower than rung 5 (\"Require parental permission before staff use a different name or pronouns\"), and the bill also has a separate funding section.\n\n`needs_source` asks for her own words on H.R. 2616, such as a floor statement or press release. I kept one quote, the op-ed sentence about the ruling, tiered `direction`.","ttft_ms":16874,"type":"result","duration_ms":39790,"uuid":"5e6431ca-5761-4401-b8d1-a27d8e80ed9b","ttft_stream_ms":1688,"time_to_request_ms":294,"first_content_frame_ms":1688,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
